{{Nihongo|'''Daisuke Ejima'''|江島 大佑|Ejima Daisuke|born January 13, 1986 in [[Kyoto Prefecture]]}}<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.ssu.co.jp/service/en/sunnysideup/ssupeople/daisuke-ejima/ |title=Daisuke Ejima |website=Sunny Side Up Group |access-date=2026-07-19}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.ninomiyasports.com/archives/71460 |title=江島大佑（パラ水泳）第１回「やっぱり水泳が好きだった」 |website=Sports Communications |language=ja |date=2017-11-05 |access-date=2026-07-19}}</ref> is a retired [[Paralympic swimmer]] from Japan competing mainly in category [[S7 (classification)|S7]] events.

Ejima has competed at two Paralympics, firstly in [[2004 Summer Paralympics|2004]] in Athens and then in [[2008 Summer Paralympics|2008]] in Beijing.  In Athens he was part of the Japanese [[Swimming at the 2004 Summer Paralympics – Men's 4 × 50 metre medley relay 20pts|4×50m Medley]] team that won a silver medal behind a new Paralympic record set by Brazil.  He also competed in the [[Swimming at the 2004 Summer Paralympics – Men's 50 metre butterfly S7|50m butterfly]] finishing seventh, [[Swimming at the 2004 Summer Paralympics – Men's 100 metre backstroke S7|100m backstroke]] finishing seventh and was disqualified from the heat of [[Swimming at the 2004 Summer Paralympics – Men's 200 metre individual medley SM7|200m individual medley]].  At the 2008 games he competed in the same individual events finishing fourth in the [[Swimming at the 2008 Summer Paralympics – Men's 50 metre butterfly S7|50m butterfly]], fifth in the [[Swimming at the 2008 Summer Paralympics – Men's 100 metre backstroke S7|100m backstroke]] and seventh in the [[Swimming at the 2008 Summer Paralympics – Men's 200 metre individual medley SM7|200m individual medley]]. He competed in the same categories at the [[2012 Paralympics]] in London.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.paralympic.org/daisuke-ejima |title=Daisuke Ejima |website=Paralympic.org |publisher=[[International Paralympic Committee]] |access-date=}}</ref><ref name=Nippon>{{cite web |url=https://www.nippon.com/en/japan-topics/b08908/a-life-in-water-paralympic-swimmer-ejima-daisuke-on-the-road-to-the-tokyo-games-and-beyond.html |title=A Life in Water: Paralympic Swimmer Ejima Daisuke on the Road to the Tokyo Games and Beyond |website=[[nippon.com]] |date=2019-06-05 |access-date=2026-07-19}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Ejima started taking swimming classes at the age of three, and continued to train throughout his childhood with the goal of practicing competitively. When he was in eighth grade, he had a [[stroke]] after a baseball game with friends, remaining with the left side of his body paralyzed. Over time, he partly regained motion in his left leg.<ref name=Nippon/>

